# C9.1 and C9.2: an Oqthara builtin wins over a same-named Java method on a
# collection receiver, and the rule is the same on both backends.
#
# The receiver is a real `java.util.ArrayList`, so both calls below reach the
# builtin first: `sort` refuses the two-argument comparator by arity rather than
# handing it to `List.sort`, and `remove` refuses text rather than matching
# `List.remove(Object)`.
#
# The last line is the part that makes the two refusals mean something. The list
# is `[3, 1, 2]`, not `[3, 1]`, because `reverseOrder()` leaves `[3, 1]` unchanged
# and a probe whose final line cannot move is not evidence. A `List.sort` that had
# run would reorder this to `[3, 2, 1]`, and a `List.remove(Object)` that had run
# would leave two elements; the printed `[3, 1, 2]` rules out both.
use java.util.ArrayList
use java.util.Comparator
xs = ArrayList()
xs.add(3)
xs.add(1)
xs.add(2)
try
  print xs.sort(Comparator.reverseOrder())
catch err
  print err.message
end
try
  print xs.remove("x")
catch err
  print err.message
end
print xs
